  "verdict": "UNRESOLVED",
  "verdict_reasons": [
    "DECISIVE: the two calibration shapes disagree on the SIGN of the bias at the operating point. The weak-factor shape gives -2.1453 (procedure UNDERSTATES the truth) while the asset-class-block shape gives +4.5177 (procedure OVERSTATES it). Since the real residual correlation shape is unknown -- that is precisely what is being estimated -- the bias cannot be signed, let alone corrected. Under the block shape the observed 16.9550 inverts to a true breadth BELOW the bottom of the ladder (< 11, a clear FAIL); under the weak-factor shape it inverts to ~20 (a clear PASS). The correction is a property of the assumed shape, not of the estimator.",
    "NULL-FIDELITY FAILURE: on real data the in-sample residual breadth exceeds the PIT one by +2.0457 (19.0007 vs 16.9550), but at the inverted truth the weak-factor null predicts a gap of only +0.0102. The null does not reproduce the real data's own in-sample/PIT relationship, most plausibly because real factor loadings drift while the null's are constant. A calibration that cannot reproduce a known feature of the real data should not be used to correct it.",
    "METHOD 2 IS INTERNALLY UNSTABLE: across its split designs the raw disjoint-sample breadth ranges 14.0112 .. 18.7147, straddling the floor of 15.0. Merely reversing which half estimates and which is tested moves the answer from one side of the floor to the other, so Method 2 does not by itself settle the gate either."
  ],
  "what_this_is": "Quantification of the optimistic bias that tsmom_factor_decomposition_2026-09-07 (merged b8880c8) disclosed in its own 16.9550 PIT residual-breadth figure. Two methods: a calibrated noise-injection null and a disjoint-sample estimator. Measurement only -- no TSMOM signal, no DSR, no preservation_score, no registration, no live-status change, nothing pushed."
